.TH FXSOUND 1 "2026-09-21" "fxsound 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fxsound \- system-wide audio enhancement for PipeWire
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fxsound
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.br
.B fxsound
.B \-\-status
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B fxsound
.B \-\-watch
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-meters ]
.br
.B fxsound
.B \-\-self\-test
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fxsound
presents itself to PipeWire as a virtual sink, processes everything
applications play into it \(en a ten-band equalizer plus fidelity, ambience,
surround, dynamic boost and bass \(en and forwards the result to the real
output device. A second, independent lane does the same for a microphone: it
runs a voice chain (denoise, high-pass, downward expander, equalizer,
de-esser, compressor, makeup gain, look-ahead limiter) and publishes a virtual
source. Both lanes can run at once; each has a device, a preset and a power
state of its own, and either can be detached.
.PP
Run with no options,
.B fxsound
starts and shows its window. If an instance is already running, the second
process does not start a second instance: it forwards its command line to the
first over a control socket in
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/fxsound ,
raises that instance's window and exits. Every option below therefore works
both as a startup option and as a way to drive an instance that is already
running.
.PP
The exceptions are
.BR \-\-status " and " \-\-watch ,
which print and exit without raising anything, and
.BR \-\-self\-test ,
which never contacts a running instance at all.
.PP
This forwarding is what makes keyboard shortcuts possible. A Wayland client
cannot grab global shortcuts, by design, so the compositor owns the binding and
calls
.B fxsound
with the matching option; see
.B EXAMPLES
below. A status bar or a compositor script that would rather not spawn a
process per query can use the D-Bus interface instead; see
.BR D\-BUS .
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Processing
.TP
.BI \-\-power= 0|1|toggle
Turn audio processing on or off for the edit direction (see
.BR \-\-edit ).
Any non-zero integer means on;
.B toggle
inverts the current state.
.TP
.BI \-\-set_effect= NAME:VALUE[,NAME:VALUE...]
Set effect levels on the slider scale,
.RB 0 \(en 10 .
Accepted names are
.BR fidelity " (also " clarity "), " ambience ", " surround ", "
.BR dynamicboost " (also " dynamic_boost ") and " bass " (also"
.BR bassboost ", " bass_boost ).
At most five pairs, since there are five effects. The music effects apply to
the output lane only; a microphone uses the voice chain instead.
.TP
.BI \-\-num_bands= N
Number of equalizer bands. Only 5, 10, 15, 20 and 31 are accepted; anything
else is an error rather than a silent fallback.
.TP
.BI \-\-set_band_freq= BAND:HZ[,BAND:HZ...]
Move band centre frequencies. Band indices are 0-based. The outer bound is
20\(en20000 Hz; the usable range for any one band also depends on its
neighbours and is enforced by the running instance.
.TP
.BI \-\-set_band_gain= BAND:DB[,BAND:DB...]
Set band gains in dB, 0-based band indices, gain
.RB \-12 \(en+12 .
.TP
.BI \-\-filter_q= Q
Equalizer filter Q,
.RB 1.0 \(en 3.0 ,
rounded to the nearest 0.5 \(en the step size of the equivalent slider.
.TP
.BI \-\-balance= DB
Stereo balance,
.RB \-20 \(en+20
dB, rounded to a whole number. Positive pans right.
.TP
.BI \-\-master_gain= DB
Master gain,
.RB \-20 \(en+20
dB, rounded to a whole number.
.TP
.BI \-\-volume_leveling= AMOUNT
Volume levelling amount,
.RB 0.0 \(en 4.0 ,
rounded to the nearest 0.5. The single-l American spelling is the Windows
build's and is kept verbatim.
.TP
.BI \-\-noise\-suppression= LEVEL
Noise suppression for the microphone lane, as a control surface over RNNoise:
.BR off ", " light ", " medium " or " strong ,
or
.B preset
to follow whatever the selected voice preset asks for, which is the default.
The setting outlives the preset: pick a level here and every voice preset runs
at it until it is set back to
.BR preset .
.SS Presets
.TP
.BI \-\-preset= NAME
Select a preset by its exact, case-sensitive name, in the edit direction's
list: a
.I .fac
preset for the output lane, a voice preset for the input lane. An unknown name
is an error, not a silent no-op.
.TP
.BI \-\-save_preset= NAME
Save the current modified settings as a new user preset.
.TP
.B \-\-overwrite_preset
Overwrite the selected user preset with its unsaved changes.
.TP
.B \-\-undo_preset
Discard the selected preset's unsaved changes.
.TP
.BI \-\-rename_preset= NAME
Rename the selected user preset.
.TP
.B \-\-delete_preset
Delete the selected user preset.
.PP
A preset name becomes a
.I .fac
filename, and preset files are meant to travel between Linux and Windows, so
the Windows reserved characters
.RB ( < " " > " " : " " \(dq " " / " " \e " " | " " ? " " * )
are stripped from a name and the result is truncated to 64 characters. Voice
presets follow the same rule with a
.I .toml
extension.
.SS Devices and directions
.TP
.BI \-\-output= DEVICE|off
Select the playback device the output lane renders to, by its exact name. The
name is the PipeWire
.IR node.description ;
.I node.name
is accepted too, because that is what the settings file persists.
.B off
detaches the lane: the virtual sink stays published and nothing is rendered
until a device is chosen again. Picking a device also makes that lane the edit
direction. For compatibility with 0.3.0, naming a microphone here is treated as
.B \-\-input
with a note on standard error.
.TP
.BI \-\-input= DEVICE|off
The same for the microphone lane: select the capture device the voice chain
reads from, or detach the lane with
.BR off .
.TP
.BI \-\-edit= output|input
Choose the edit direction: the lane the window shows and the preset, effect
and power options above act on. The window's device combo and the tray menu
change it too. Without this option a forwarded command line acts on whichever
direction the instance is already editing.
.TP
.BI \-\-view= 1|2
Switch the window between the Lite (1) and Pro (2) layouts.
.TP
.BI \-\-language= CODE
Display language, for example
.BR en ", " ru ", " fr .
.TP
.BR \-\-run_minimized ", " \-\-hide
Start without showing the window, or hide the window of a running instance.
This is what the systemd user unit and the autostart entry invoke.
.TP
.B \-\-show
Show and raise the window of the running instance.
.SS Shortcut targets
.PP
These exist so a compositor keybinding has something to call. They have no
Windows counterpart, where the application registers global hotkeys itself.
.TP
.B \-\-toggle\-power
Toggle audio processing in the edit direction.
.TP
.B \-\-toggle\-window
Show the window if it is hidden, hide it if it is showing \(en the same toggle
a left click on the tray icon performs.
.TP
.B \-\-next\-preset
Select the next preset of the edit direction, wrapping.
.TP
.B \-\-prev\-preset
Select the previous preset of the edit direction, wrapping.
.TP
.B \-\-next\-output
Cycle the output lane to the next usable playback device, wrapping. Playback
devices only: it never crosses into the microphones.
.TP
.B \-\-next\-input
Cycle the microphone lane to the next usable capture device, wrapping.
.SS Control
.TP
.B \-\-status
Print the running instance's state on standard output and exit, one
.I key: value
line per item: the version, whether processing is on, then
.BR output ", " input ", " output_preset ", " input_preset " and " direction
(the edit direction), followed by an
.B input:
block with the voice chain's telemetry. Every other option on the same
command line is ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the
.BR \-\-status ", " \-\-watch " or " \-\-self\-test
report as JSON instead of as lines: one object for
.B \-\-status
(with
.BR output " and " input
objects and an
.B input_meters
block), one object per line for the other two. Not the default: the line
format is what existing scripts already grep.
.TP
.B \-\-watch
Subscribe to the running instance and print one event per line until it
quits or standard output is closed. The first event is
.B status
(the full
.B \-\-status
document); after that
.BR power ", " preset_changed ", " device_changed ", " devices_changed ", "
.BR audio_state ", " direction ", " notice ", " window " and " quit
as they happen. Plain output is
.I event key=value ...
per line; with
.B \-\-json
each line is one object carrying
.BR \(dqv\(dq:1 ", " \(dqevent\(dq " and " \(dqts\(dq
(milliseconds since the epoch) plus the event's fields. This is what a status
bar module should use instead of polling
.BR \-\-status .
.TP
.B \-\-meters
With
.BR \-\-watch ,
also stream the microphone lane's meters as
.B input_meters
events, at most four per second. Off by default because a bar that only wants
to know the preset name should not be woken that often.
.TP
.B \-\-self\-test
Check the installation and exit: the version; that the factory
.I .fac
presets and the voice presets are found under the install prefix and parse;
that the settings file parses; an offline run of both processing chains with
RNNoise instantiated; that the desktop entry, the icons (application and
status), the systemd unit and this manual page are present; that the runtime
directory can be created. PipeWire and the session bus are reported as
.B skip
rather than as failures when there is none, so the test is meaningful in a
container with no session. It runs before the single-instance lock is taken,
so it works beside a running FxSound and never forwards anything to it. The
exit status is 0 if and only if every check that was not skipped passed.
.TP
.B \-\-quit
Quit the running instance, autosaving a modified preset first.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.PP
Options whose Windows spelling uses underscores also accept the hyphenated
form:
.B \-\-num\-bands
and
.B \-\-num_bands
are the same option. The hyphenated spellings are hidden from
.BR \-\-help .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. For a forwarded command line, this means the running instance accepted
it, not that the effect is audible yet. For
.BR \-\-self\-test ,
every check that ran passed.
.TP
.B 1
.B \-\-self\-test
found at least one failing check; the report says which.
.TP
.B 2
The command line could not be parsed \(en an unknown option, or a value outside
its range.
.PP
Any other non-zero status is a failure to start or a failure to reach a running
instance.
.SH D\-BUS
A running instance also serves an interface on the session bus, for status
bars, compositor scripts and anything else that would rather hold a connection
than spawn a process per query. The command line stays on the control socket
so that it keeps working without a session bus; both paths run exactly the
same commands, so they cannot disagree.
.TP
Bus names
.B org.fxsound.FxSound
(the API) and
.B com.fxsound.FxSound
(the desktop-entry id, so a shell can associate the window with it).
.TP
Object path
.I /org/fxsound/FxSound
.TP
Interface
.B org.fxsound.FxSound
.PP
Methods:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
TogglePower() \(-> b        SetPower(b)
NextPreset()               PrevPreset()
SetPreset(s)               GetPreset() \(-> s
SetOutput(s)               SetInput(s)
NextOutput()               NextInput()
SetNoiseSuppression(s)     SetEditDirection(s)
GetStatus() \(-> s          (the \-\-status \-\-json document)
Show()                     Hide()
ToggleWindow()             Quit()
.EE
.RE
.PP
Properties:
.BR Version " (s, constant), " Power " (b), " Preset " (s), "
.BR Output " (s), " Input " (s) and " Direction " (s)."
.PP
Signals:
.BR PowerChanged (b),
.BR PresetChanged (s\ direction,\ s\ name),
.BR DeviceChanged (s\ direction,\ s\ node_name,\ s\ description),
.BR AudioStateChanged (s\ json)
and
.BR Notice (s\ message).
.PP
If the name is already taken on the bus, the instance logs that and serves the
control socket only. With
.BR busctl (1):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
busctl \-\-user call org.fxsound.FxSound /org/fxsound/FxSound \e
    org.fxsound.FxSound SetPreset s Rock
busctl \-\-user get\-property org.fxsound.FxSound /org/fxsound/FxSound \e
    org.fxsound.FxSound Power
busctl \-\-user monitor org.fxsound.FxSound
.EE
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Holds the instance lock and the control socket. When the session provides none,
.IB /tmp/fxsound- uid
is used instead.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Settings live under
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fxsound ,
defaulting to
.IR ~/.config/fxsound .
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
User presets live under
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/fxsound/presets ,
defaulting to
.IR ~/.local/share/fxsound/presets .
.TP
.B DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS
Where the D-Bus interface is served. Unset, the instance runs socket-only and
.B \-\-self\-test
reports the bus as skipped.
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Log filter, in the usual
.B env_logger
syntax, for example
.BR RUST_LOG=debug .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/fxsound/presets/Factsoft/
Factory presets, in the
.I .fac
format shared with the Windows build.
.TP
.I /usr/share/fxsound/presets/BonusPresets/
Bonus presets, same format.
.TP
.I /usr/share/fxsound/presets/Input/
Voice presets for the microphone chain, in TOML. They are TOML rather than
.I .fac
because the
.I .fac
format is a byte-for-byte contract with the Windows build and has nowhere to
put a gate threshold.
.PP
Only
.I /usr
and
.I /usr/local
are searched for these directories. An install under any other prefix starts
with an empty preset list.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/fxsound/presets/
Imported and user-saved presets; voice presets under
.IR Input/ .
.TP
.I ~/.config/fxsound/settings.toml
Settings. A corrupt file is never fatal: the defaults are used and the audio
still comes up.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/fxsound/instance.lock
Holds the running instance's pid under an exclusive
.BR flock (2).
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/fxsound/instance.sock
The control socket. One JSON line per request, one per reply; a
.B \-\-watch
connection stays open and receives one JSON line per event.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/fxsound.service
A systemd user unit, installed but not enabled. See
.B NOTES
below.
.TP
.I /usr/share/icons/hicolor/scalable/status/com.fxsound.FxSound\-{off,on,processing}.svg
The tray icon's three states, looked up by name by the StatusNotifier host.
.SH EXAMPLES
Mute the processing from a compositor keybinding, in Hyprland:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
bind = CTRL SHIFT, Q, exec, fxsound \-\-toggle\-power
bind = CTRL SHIFT, E, exec, fxsound \-\-toggle\-window
bind = CTRL SHIFT, A, exec, fxsound \-\-next\-preset
.EE
.RE
.PP
A worked set of these, with the window rules, is installed at
.IR /usr/share/doc/fxsound\-linux/hyprland.conf.example
.RI ( examples/hyprland.conf.example
on Debian).
.PP
Ask a running instance what it is doing:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
fxsound \-\-status \-\-json | jq .
.EE
.RE
.PP
Feed a status bar without polling: one JSON object per line, starting with
the full status.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
fxsound \-\-watch \-\-json | jq \-\-unbuffered \-r '.event'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Point the microphone lane at a headset and quieten a fan:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
fxsound \-\-input='Jabra Evolve2' \-\-preset='Gaming Headset' \e
    \-\-noise\-suppression=strong
.EE
.RE
.PP
Select a preset and lift the bass on the speakers without opening the window:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
fxsound \-\-edit=output \-\-preset=Rock \-\-set_effect=bass:7,ambience:3
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check an installation, for example from a package's post-install test:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
fxsound \-\-self\-test \-\-json
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
The systemd user unit is installed but deliberately not enabled. The desktop
entry already starts FxSound when you launch it, and an enabled user unit on
top of that puts two processes in a race for the control socket; the loser
exits, but which one that is depends on the session's startup order. To have
FxSound come up with the session instead of on demand:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
systemctl \-\-user enable \-\-now fxsound
.EE
.RE
.PP
Switching the session's default sink onto the FxSound sink is a session-manager
operation. Without
.BR wireplumber (1)
or an equivalent, the sink appears in the graph and nothing moves onto it.
.PP
The preset import and export dialogs are XDG portal calls. They need a portal
backend that implements the file chooser (the GTK, GNOME or KDE one);
wlroots' own backend does not.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pipewire (1),
.BR wireplumber (1),
.BR pw\-cli (1),
.BR busctl (1),
.BR systemctl (1)
.PP
.I /usr/share/doc/fxsound\-linux/README.md
(gzipped on Debian and Ubuntu).
